00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.330
And quickly though, before
I do the new features,

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:53.390
I want to quickly baseline
on what Cloud Dataproc is.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:57.140
So Cloud Dataproc, this
is Google Cloud Platform's

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:01.770
fully managed Apache Spark
and Apache Hadoop service.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.879
And I'm saying Hadoop and
Spark, but it's really

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:08.300
an engine for running lots
of open-source software

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.500
in that ecosystem.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:12.630
We give you customizable
machine types,

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:16.130
meaning that you can have
a set of machine learning

00:01:16.130 --> 00:01:18.510
jobs running on one cluster
that might be very compute

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.360
intensive, while
at the same time,

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.380
you have some ad hoc BI
applications that are running

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.300
on a memory-intensive cluster.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:29.000
And the two never have
to contend for resources

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:33.470
but can still be reading and
writing from the same cloud

00:01:33.470 --> 00:01:35.462
storage data sets.

00:01:35.462 --> 00:01:36.920
And we also had
some customers that

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:39.620
create very large
customers with us

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:43.085
when they need
clusters like this.

00:01:43.085 --> 00:01:45.210
And they can just take them
down when they're done.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.920
So for instance, our engineers
were working with Twitter,

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.890
and they received the
note that was like, hey,

00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:53.963
are we your largest
Dataproc cluster?

00:01:53.963 --> 00:01:55.380
And we kind of
look at each other,

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:57.630
we're like, I don't know,
what are you talking about?

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:58.670
And we went and checked.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:03.200
And they had actually
needed a 90,000 core cluster

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:06.260
and were able to go,
create that on demand,

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.190
never getting a hold
of the Dataproc team,

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:09.680
worrying about procurement.

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:12.560
Just went, created a
90,000-core Dataproc cluster,

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.880
and then tore it down when
they were done with it.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:18.290
And so they were able to do this
because we have a lot of tools

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:21.080
and features that are
specifically designed

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.300
for supporting these
kind of architectures

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:27.220
and ephemeral Hadoop and
Spark clusters in general.

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:29.360
And we give you a lot of
tight integration back

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:31.280
to the rest of Google Cloud.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:35.210
So we are not just giving you
yet another cluster that you're

00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:36.860
going to have to go manage.

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:39.620
We are letting you run
all of your existing

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:43.490
open-source software but
still modernizing your stack

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:45.610
as we do this.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:49.250
And we give our Cloud Dataproc
customers a lot of flexibility

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:52.070
in how they choose to
run the Dataproc service.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:53.680
So we exposed a lot of knobs.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:57.190
Now, we try to put sane
defaults on those knobs.

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:58.690
If you don't want
to deal with that,

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:00.940
you can just go and get
started very easily.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:03.130
But those knobs are
exposed, and so we

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:05.860
have customers that use
Dataproc in a couple

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:08.330
of different models.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:12.560
The first model is this idea
of a job-scoped cluster.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.660
And this is really effective
for our Cloud Dataproc customers

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.720
with batch or ETL-type jobs.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:23.380
Now in this mode, you
can have a single command

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:26.520
that will send a graph of
jobs to Cloud Dataproc.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:31.690
We will spin up a right-sized
cluster, run those jobs,

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:34.120
make sure that cluster
gets torn down,

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:36.370
and then save all
of that information

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:39.060
about what happened, all
the logging and audits,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.310
into Stackdriver for you.

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:44.970
And this works really
well with Cloud Dataproc

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:48.750
because we can usually give you
a fully-loaded Hadoop and Spark

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:52.000
cluster in around 90 seconds.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:54.960
So when you only have a
minute and a half overhead

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.270
to create in a
cluster, you can start

00:03:57.270 --> 00:04:01.533
to think about jobs and clusters
almost as a single entity.

00:04:01.533 --> 00:04:02.950
And we have a lot
of features that

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:05.450
are going to help you with this
model-- things like our jobs

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:10.610
API, workflow templates, and
cluster scheduled deletion.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.460
But we also have
customers that tell us

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.220
they have scenarios for
long-running Cloud Dataproc

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.630
clusters.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:21.149
And these might be
scenarios for things like,

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:24.980
if there's a shared interactive
analysis with Jupyter

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:28.640
or Zeppelin notebooks,
or maybe they're

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.830
building BI applications
based on Druid.

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.390
And we have plenty of features,
like our high availability mode

00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:38.210
and our auto-scaler, that are
going to help with this model

00:04:38.210 --> 00:04:39.110
too.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.570
And if you want to learn
more, Mikayla and I actually

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:46.520
did a top 10 tips article on
the Google Data Analytics Blog

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:48.170
a couple months
back you can find.

00:04:48.170 --> 00:04:50.550
And she even has a webinar
associated with that,

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:52.340
so there's a lot of
materials out there

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:54.840
if you want to know more about
how to run longstanding Cloud

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:57.170
Dataproc clusters.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:00.500
But regardless of the
model that you choose,

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:05.870
our goal with Cloud Dataproc is
changing open-source software

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.210
to be cloud-native.

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:13.230
This means making features there
are going to be fast, easy,

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.390
and cost effective.

00:05:15.390 --> 00:05:18.870
Last year alone, we put
more than 30 new features

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:22.410
into Cloud Dataproc, and
so this is still an area

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:25.268
Google is heavily investing in.

00:05:25.268 --> 00:05:26.810
And so now let's
jump in and actually

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:28.727
start talking about some
of these new features

00:05:28.727 --> 00:05:31.108
and what's going on
in Cloud Dataproc.

00:05:31.108 --> 00:05:32.900
So I'm going to start
by talking about some

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:35.000
of the recent
open-sourced software

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:39.220
we've recently integrated
with Cloud Dataproc.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:40.720
Now the theme to
all these features

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:44.110
that I'm about to talk
about is that Cloud Dataproc

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:48.610
lets you take the open-source
tools, algorithms,

00:05:48.610 --> 00:05:52.720
and programming languages that
you're already using today

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:58.960
but make it really easy to apply
them to cloud-scaled data sets

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:02.050
while at the same time
helping you integrate

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:04.390
with the entire GCP ecosystem.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.550
We've just recently launched a
new version of Cloud Dataproc.

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:13.820
It's now generally
available, version 1.4.

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:16.070
The headliner on this
version is probably

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:20.480
Spark 2.4, and that comes with
a lot of usability and stability

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.940
updates, and that's something
that you can really start

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:25.292
to combine with Cloud Dataproc.

00:06:25.292 --> 00:06:26.750
We also had a lot
of customers that

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:29.360
told us, hey, we
love Cloud Dataproc,

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:31.250
but we've built so
much of our enterprise

00:06:31.250 --> 00:06:34.520
tooling and internal
standards around Ubuntu.

00:06:34.520 --> 00:06:39.170
So we went back and we
recreated Dataproc on Ubuntu,

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:42.920
and that exposed a new
knob, a choice of image

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:46.850
or operating system
type for Dataproc.

00:06:46.850 --> 00:06:48.650
Now, for years,
we've had customers

00:06:48.650 --> 00:06:51.800
that have built very tight
and sophisticated security

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:57.980
controls with Cloud IAM, and
VM, and cluster isolation.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:06:59.930
But we've also
had customers that

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:02.750
wanted the open-source tools,
like Kerberos, built directly

00:07:02.750 --> 00:07:05.600
into Dataproc,
which is why we now

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:10.390
have a beta version of Kerberos
integrated with Dataproc.

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:12.950
And this is going to let
you do things like tie back

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.500
your Dataproc users
to Active Directory,

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:17.090
or it makes it a
simple button click

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:22.640
to turn on Hadoop secure mode,
or add encryption in-flight.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:26.420
Tomorrow at 9:00 AM, I'm going
to be speaking with Cloudera

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:27.680
on securing your data lakes.

00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:31.550
And essentially, we're going to
have a 55-minute presentation

00:07:31.550 --> 00:07:32.430
on this entire slide.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:33.847
So if you are
interested in what's

00:07:33.847 --> 00:07:36.320
going on in Hadoop security,
I would recommend that session

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:38.750
tomorrow morning.

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:41.980
But back to Dataproc
itself, when

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:44.270
you create a
Dataproc cluster, you

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:49.170
can specify what we call
an initialization action.

00:07:49.170 --> 00:07:52.770
That's an executable or
script that Cloud Dataproc's

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:55.350
going to run on all
nodes of your cluster

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:59.020
immediately after
that cluster spins up.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:02.290
Now, we talked to a lot of our
customers and we asked them--

00:08:02.290 --> 00:08:05.950
we tried to identify the most
common open-source software

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:08.395
that's being installed
on Cloud Dataproc.

00:08:08.395 --> 00:08:09.770
And then what we
want to do is we

00:08:09.770 --> 00:08:11.812
try to provide starter
scripts, so everyone's not

00:08:11.812 --> 00:08:14.570
rewriting that same code, and
those are the initialization

00:08:14.570 --> 00:08:19.500
actions that we put into
our GitHub repository.

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:21.930
Some of the more recent
initialization actions

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.510
include Apache Ranger.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.850
And we find a lot
of customers, when

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:28.680
they start with
Cloud Dataproc, they

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:33.270
might be starting with a pretty
monolithic on-prem cluster

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:37.740
that's sharing resources
between ad hoc BI analysts, who

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.299
are trying to run
their SQL queries

00:08:39.299 --> 00:08:41.250
and waiting for those
queries to come back,

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:44.310
along with a lot of
scheduled and batch jobs that

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:46.720
have piled up over the
last couple of years.

00:08:46.720 --> 00:08:48.540
And so one of the first
ways that customers

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:50.250
start to migrate to
the cloud is they

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:52.710
take a lot of those
batch and scheduled jobs

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:55.110
and start putting them
into Cloud Dataproc

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:59.310
to free up that on-prem
cluster for the analyst.

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.030
And we've had
customers that said,

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:04.270
hey, the code, it maps over
great, everything runs,

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:07.230
but I would really love to keep
all of my existing security

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:10.140
controls and policies without
redeveloping all of that.

00:09:10.140 --> 00:09:12.540
And so that's where something
like the Apache Ranger

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:14.160
initialization action
can really help

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:16.890
get them started doing that.

00:09:16.890 --> 00:09:19.470
We've also recently created
an initialization action

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:21.390
for HBase.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:23.070
And while we do,
at Google Cloud,

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:27.930
have Bigtable, which is a
NoSQL store with HBase API

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:32.260
compatibility, customers,
they've told us,

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:34.530
hey, sometimes I just
need code processors,

00:09:34.530 --> 00:09:37.290
or I want to run Apache
Phoenix with SQL,

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:40.420
and so that's why we came
out with Apache HBase.

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:42.900
We also have the
fish that you see

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:46.230
there, that stands for
TonY, or TensorFlow on YARN.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.500
And that's a project
that we did with LinkedIn

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:51.270
that makes it easy
to run TensorFlow

00:09:51.270 --> 00:09:54.780
jobs on your Dataproc cluster
without having to move over

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:57.840
to a separate API, like CMLE.

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.600
You can just call it directly
from your Spark workflows

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:02.860
and have TensorFlow scale.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:05.640
And there's actually a full
Next session tomorrow at 11:40

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:07.473
that you might want to
check out if you want

00:10:07.473 --> 00:10:09.445
to learn more about that one.

00:10:09.445 --> 00:10:11.820
But, again, the theme here--
you're probably sitting here

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:13.620
staring at all of
these open-source logos

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:15.570
and you might be thinking,
hey, doesn't Google

00:10:15.570 --> 00:10:18.672
have a fully-managed offering
for some of this stuff?

00:10:18.672 --> 00:10:19.380
And you're right.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:20.940
Oftentimes they do.

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:23.010
But Cloud Dataproc,
our goal is just

00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:25.590
to make it easy to run
that open-source version

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:30.060
of the software if
that's your preference.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:32.640
Now we've also recently had some
optional components that got

00:10:32.640 --> 00:10:35.345
promoted to either GA or beta.

00:10:35.345 --> 00:10:36.720
And an optional
component, that's

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:39.600
what happens when
initialization actions,

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:41.820
it reaches enough
popularity that we

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:45.775
decide we're going to make this
a first-class Dataproc citizen.

00:10:45.775 --> 00:10:47.400
And optional component,
that's software

00:10:47.400 --> 00:10:50.490
that you can just add
with a simple checkbox,

00:10:50.490 --> 00:10:53.250
and that becomes part
of the Dataproc image.

00:10:53.250 --> 00:10:56.350
And so that gives you the
fast clusters spin-up time,

00:10:56.350 --> 00:10:59.160
but it also gets
you fully integrated

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:00.492
with all of Dataproc.

00:11:00.492 --> 00:11:01.950
And if you want to
know what I mean

00:11:01.950 --> 00:11:04.440
by fully integrated,
that's something,

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:06.480
go talk to your
cluster admin about.

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.220
They probably know
what it's like to try

00:11:08.220 --> 00:11:10.770
to keep up with all
the latest versions

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:12.690
of these various
open-source software

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:14.370
and keep everything in sync.

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:18.510
You install the latest
version of Spark,

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:21.795
a butterfly flaps its wings, it
steps on some Java dependency

00:11:21.795 --> 00:11:23.670
over in Zeppelin, and
now all of a sudden all

00:11:23.670 --> 00:11:25.500
your class paths are misaligned.

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:28.110
That's the kind of
things that, because we

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:32.160
bake the optional components
into the image itself,

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:34.560
that ensures that
this whole menagerie

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:39.460
of open-source software is
actually working in tandem.

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:42.040
And a new optional component
we just put into alpha

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:45.190
is Druid, a
column-oriented open-source

00:11:45.190 --> 00:11:46.600
distributed data store.

00:11:46.600 --> 00:11:49.570
You'll most often find
this with OLAP applications

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:51.980
on really high volume data.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:56.170
So one common use-case that I
keep seeing with GCP customers

00:11:56.170 --> 00:11:58.630
is they're taking
something like an Apache

00:11:58.630 --> 00:12:02.200
Kafka or a Confluent
incoming stream,

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:07.180
they land all of that data into
a Google Cloud Storage sync,

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:10.150
and then folks want
to see dashboards

00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:12.850
on that data about what's
coming in in real time.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:15.490
And they might want that daily,
they might want that hourly.

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:17.590
And so they can schedule
a Cloud Dataproc cluster

00:12:17.590 --> 00:12:21.670
to spin up, run
those aggregations,

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:24.550
upsert that into a long-running
Cloud Dataproc cluster running

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:27.130
Druid, and then tear
down that cluster that

00:12:27.130 --> 00:12:29.680
was running those jobs.

00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:31.210
And then you have
your application

00:12:31.210 --> 00:12:32.920
for the dashboarding
or whatever,

00:12:32.920 --> 00:12:36.250
going against that OLAP Cloud
Dataproc store running Druid.

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:42.440
The Dataproc Jobs API,
we have a new SparkR Job

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:44.130
type available for that--

00:12:44.130 --> 00:12:45.950
now the Dataproc Jobs API.

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:47.750
That's what makes it
possible to submit

00:12:47.750 --> 00:12:50.300
a job to an existing
Cloud Dataproc

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:54.650
cluster with jobs.submit
call over HTTP.

00:12:54.650 --> 00:12:57.050
Or if you're on a
command line interface,

00:12:57.050 --> 00:13:00.140
you can use GCloud, or even
in the Google Cloud Platform

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:02.100
console itself.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:04.940
So now it's possible to
submit your SparkR Jobs

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:08.550
through that Jobs API, which
has them logged and monitored,

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:10.550
and that makes it really
easy to build automated

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:13.328
tooling around your R code.

00:13:13.328 --> 00:13:14.870
It also makes it
pretty easy to start

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:16.820
separating the
permissions of who

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:20.630
has access to the cluster versus
who has access just to submit

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:22.440
jobs against the cluster.

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:25.402
And you can do that now without
setting up gateway nodes

00:13:25.402 --> 00:13:26.360
or something like Livy.

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:27.890
You can just use the Jobs API.

00:13:30.610 --> 00:13:33.490
And a new feature that just came
out is this Component Gateway.

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:36.880
And you see what it does here
is, I can now, with one button

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:39.850
click, enable the Component
Gateway, come into the web

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:42.950
interfaces, and it
will expose the web

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:45.370
UIs associated with
the component that

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:47.050
are running on the cluster.

00:13:47.050 --> 00:13:52.150
And I can click into those based
on my cloud IAM permissions.

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:55.600
And again, this is a new
feature along the same lines

00:13:55.600 --> 00:13:57.970
of the Jobs API,
where we want to make

00:13:57.970 --> 00:14:01.660
it easy to access
Dataproc without a bunch

00:14:01.660 --> 00:14:05.650
of sophisticated edge
nodes or networking setup.

00:14:05.650 --> 00:14:07.420
We want to make it
easy that you can

00:14:07.420 --> 00:14:11.230
use GCP as your
security perimeter

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:14.040
for some of the more
common scenarios.

00:14:14.040 --> 00:14:17.240
So Component Gateway, it was
built on top of Apache Knox.

00:14:17.240 --> 00:14:20.940
And it reverse-proxies
and exposes the web UIs.

00:14:20.940 --> 00:14:24.012
And then as long as you have the
proper cloud IAM permissions,

00:14:24.012 --> 00:14:25.220
you can directly access them.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:32.980
So a lot of clouds, when
they build their connectors,

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:34.900
they will keep them internal.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:39.430
So you have to be using their
services to take advantage

00:14:39.430 --> 00:14:41.800
of the first-class connector.

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:45.370
And the open-source
community, they've

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:47.950
done a great job of
responding to that,

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:50.020
and they started building
their own connectors.

00:14:50.020 --> 00:14:52.450
But what we saw is that
created both a lot of friction

00:14:52.450 --> 00:14:55.530
in the community as well as
confusion from customers about,

00:14:55.530 --> 00:14:59.290
hey, what connection
should I really be using?

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:03.210
So at Google, we decided to
take a different approach.

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:05.310
All of the connectors
that we build from Hadoop

00:15:05.310 --> 00:15:09.300
into the Google Cloud Platform,
those are fully open source.

00:15:09.300 --> 00:15:12.180
So we chose to work with
the open-source community

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:13.920
instead of against them.

00:15:13.920 --> 00:15:16.410
And our connectors,
they're also built

00:15:16.410 --> 00:15:19.270
with these cross-team
collaborations at Google.

00:15:19.270 --> 00:15:21.030
So our Google Cloud
Storage connector,

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:22.447
that is something
that we did hand

00:15:22.447 --> 00:15:24.690
in hand with the
Cloud Storage team

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:27.360
to make sure that you're getting
the optimal performance out

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:28.770
of these connectors.

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.050
So to tell you more about
some of the new and improved

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:32.330
connectors that
are available, I'm

00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:33.900
going to bring up Karthik,
who's actually one

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:35.150
of the engineers on that team.

00:15:39.470 --> 00:15:42.570
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: So
Dataproc is really the bridge--

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:44.950
like we see it as the bridge
between the Hadoop world

00:15:44.950 --> 00:15:45.910
and Google Cloud.

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:48.100
So to kind of be clear
on what connectors means,

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:50.410
the idea is that you can
still store your data

00:15:50.410 --> 00:15:52.660
in services like BigQuery
or Google Cloud Storage that

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:54.940
can be used through other
cloud platform tools

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:57.910
but, at the same time, process
your data through tools

00:15:57.910 --> 00:16:02.910
that you're already familiar
with in your Hadoop ecosystem.

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:03.655
So the first--

00:16:03.655 --> 00:16:05.530
I'm going to go through
a few new connectors.

00:16:05.530 --> 00:16:06.790
And this is the
first one, I think

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:07.930
this is really the coolest.

00:16:07.930 --> 00:16:11.440
It's a set of new
BigQuery read connectors.

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:13.270
So the old BigQuery
connectors, the way

00:16:13.270 --> 00:16:17.080
it worked is it would export
data from BigQuery into GCS,

00:16:17.080 --> 00:16:19.555
and then your job would
read the data from GCS.

00:16:19.555 --> 00:16:21.430
What that meant is that
your cluster actually

00:16:21.430 --> 00:16:24.160
sat idle for maybe
up to minutes as

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:25.870
that data was being exported.

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:27.530
Through this new
BigQuery storage API,

00:16:27.530 --> 00:16:30.040
we can actually stream data
directly from BigQuery,

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:32.530
and BigQuery actually
intelligently dynamically

00:16:32.530 --> 00:16:35.020
rebalances between map tasks
so that you don't end up

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:36.645
with [INAUDIBLE] and
the tasks actually

00:16:36.645 --> 00:16:38.350
finish about the same time.

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:40.863
And we can also push down
predicate filters and column

00:16:40.863 --> 00:16:42.280
filters into
BigQuery so that way,

00:16:42.280 --> 00:16:45.250
you reduce the amount of data
that is actually read out.

00:16:45.250 --> 00:16:47.270
And finally, the last
major improvement there,

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:48.770
as you look at Spark
in particular--

00:16:48.770 --> 00:16:50.383
that's why I put it
up on the slide--

00:16:50.383 --> 00:16:53.050
in the old connector, you'd have
to use the MapReduce API, which

00:16:53.050 --> 00:16:54.490
is very difficult to work with.

00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:56.990
But now we integrate with
the Spark data source API,

00:16:56.990 --> 00:16:59.140
so now it's just
spark.read.bigquery,

00:16:59.140 --> 00:17:01.270
give it a table name,
and you have a type data

00:17:01.270 --> 00:17:05.210
frame that's correct that you
can use in your Spark jobs.

00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:08.970
There's also a connector
for MapReduce as well.

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:11.150
So kind of similar,
there's another connector

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:12.770
as well for Cloud Spanner.

00:17:12.770 --> 00:17:14.359
It is also open
source on GitHub.

00:17:14.359 --> 00:17:15.780
Actually, all three that
I'm going to talk about

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:16.630
are on GitHub.

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:18.422
This one is still under
active development,

00:17:18.422 --> 00:17:19.838
but kind of the
same concept here.

00:17:19.838 --> 00:17:22.339
We push down filters, and it
integrates with the Spark data

00:17:22.339 --> 00:17:25.420
source API, so it's just
spark.read.formatcloud-spanner,

00:17:25.420 --> 00:17:30.042
and again, you have a correctly
typed data frame to work with.

00:17:30.042 --> 00:17:32.250
And finally, I'll run through
some quick improvements

00:17:32.250 --> 00:17:33.440
in the GCS connector.

00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:35.010
There's been a lot
of work in the GCS

00:17:35.010 --> 00:17:38.020
connector in the past year,
especially around performance.

00:17:38.020 --> 00:17:40.020
So the first big one is
really around supporting

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:42.280
columnar file formats.

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:44.280
So the idea with these
columnar file formats,

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:46.710
you might have a footer
at the end of the file.

00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:48.210
And so if you're
filtering data, you

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:49.770
might be able to
skip the entire file

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:51.780
or just read a
small section of it.

00:17:51.780 --> 00:17:56.030
So we really improved
random access patterns

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:58.880
in the GCS connector, and
that's what really makes it up

00:17:58.880 --> 00:18:02.460
to four times faster for
certain parquet workloads.

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:05.920
And the other major improvement
was around multi-threaded batch

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:06.420
requests.

00:18:06.420 --> 00:18:08.010
So if you're renaming
a large directory

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:09.468
or deleting a large
directory, that

00:18:09.468 --> 00:18:10.690
is now significantly faster.

00:18:13.102 --> 00:18:14.810
And so, again, just
to kind of reiterate,

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:16.250
the big thing here
is we're really

00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:18.083
focused on being good
open-source citizens.

00:18:18.083 --> 00:18:19.500
Instead of proprietary
connectors,

00:18:19.500 --> 00:18:21.160
these are all open
source on GitHub,

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:24.320
and the community can
also contribute to them.

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:26.070
And with that, I'll
hand it off to Mikayla

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:30.098
to talk about auto-awesomeness.

00:18:30.098 --> 00:18:31.140
MIKAYLA KONST: All right.

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:34.280
So what is auto-awesomeness?

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:36.600
So auto-awesomeness
basically means

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:38.850
that we manage your
infrastructure for you

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:40.950
so that you can just
focus on the insights.

00:18:40.950 --> 00:18:45.990
And we have a whole host of
different auto-awesome features

00:18:45.990 --> 00:18:47.140
that help you do this.

00:18:47.140 --> 00:18:51.420
So we have lots of features
that help you manage clusters.

00:18:51.420 --> 00:18:54.160
So for example, one
is scheduled deletion.

00:18:54.160 --> 00:18:57.790
So, let's say I have a
cluster and I want to say,

00:18:57.790 --> 00:19:00.510
OK, if this cluster's idle
for more than 10 minutes,

00:19:00.510 --> 00:19:02.760
I want Dataproc to just
automatically delete it

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:03.480
for you.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:06.005
So you can set that up.

00:19:06.005 --> 00:19:07.380
And then we manage
your jobs too.

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:08.910
So we have the Jobs API.

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:10.800
We have eight different
job types now,

00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:14.910
so that it's easy to submit
jobs through whatever automation

00:19:14.910 --> 00:19:16.890
that you're using.

00:19:16.890 --> 00:19:19.530
And then we also have ways
to manage clusters and jobs

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:20.190
together.

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:21.815
So on the next slide,
I'm going to talk

00:19:21.815 --> 00:19:25.410
about workflow templates.

00:19:25.410 --> 00:19:27.110
So what is a workflow template?

00:19:27.110 --> 00:19:27.920
What is a workflow?

00:19:27.920 --> 00:19:32.690
So a workflow template is a
way to specify a graph of jobs

00:19:32.690 --> 00:19:36.770
that you want to run, so any
arbitrary job dependencies,

00:19:36.770 --> 00:19:39.350
alongside the configuration
for the cluster

00:19:39.350 --> 00:19:41.120
that you want those
jobs to run on.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.370
So when you run a
workflow, what happens

00:19:43.370 --> 00:19:46.430
is Dataproc will spin up
a cluster on your behalf,

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:48.830
run the graph of
jobs on that cluster,

00:19:48.830 --> 00:19:50.645
and then tear down the cluster.

00:19:50.645 --> 00:19:52.280
And even if
something goes wrong,

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:54.410
the workflow will clean
up the cluster for you

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:56.250
so that you don't have
to worry about it.

00:19:56.250 --> 00:19:58.038
You can do all of
this with a single API

00:19:58.038 --> 00:19:59.330
call, which is really powerful.

00:20:01.870 --> 00:20:08.150
So you can actually run a
workflow directly from the UI,

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:10.670
and then you can monitor the
progress of this workflow

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:11.400
as well.

00:20:11.400 --> 00:20:14.120
So you can see the
jobs in this workflow.

00:20:14.120 --> 00:20:17.540
This is just a simple teragen,
terasort, teravalidate.

00:20:17.540 --> 00:20:21.030
The teragen job is done,
the terasort job is running,

00:20:21.030 --> 00:20:24.220
and the teravalidate job is
waiting for the terasort job

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:24.720
to finish.

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:30.380
So another auto-awesome
feature that we

00:20:30.380 --> 00:20:33.950
have for managing your
jobs, that's coming soon,

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:37.160
is the ability to see
all the logs associated

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:39.980
with a particular
job in Stackdriver,

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:41.260
logging all in one place.

00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:44.180
So you can select
a particular job

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:46.310
by job ID in just a
dropdown, and then

00:20:46.310 --> 00:20:49.040
you automatically see
all the driver logs

00:20:49.040 --> 00:20:51.620
and all the container
logs all in one place here

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:53.490
in Stackdriver.

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:55.760
And Stackdriver is not
just used for logging.

00:20:55.760 --> 00:20:57.510
It's also used for monitoring.

00:20:57.510 --> 00:21:00.770
So we have full integration with
Stackdriver monitoring as well.

00:21:00.770 --> 00:21:02.660
So we export metrics
from your cluster

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:04.880
to Stackdriver monitoring,
and that's actually

00:21:04.880 --> 00:21:08.070
how our auto-scaler makes
decisions about how to scale.

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:09.930
So to talk to you more
about auto-scaling,

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:11.930
I'm going to hand it back
to Karthik real quick.

00:21:20.528 --> 00:21:21.570
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: Yes.

00:21:21.570 --> 00:21:25.190
So I really see auto-scaling
as the premier auto-awesome

00:21:25.190 --> 00:21:27.070
feature.

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:29.980
Because auto-scaling is kind
of really the story of cloud,

00:21:29.980 --> 00:21:33.340
the idea that you just pay for
the resources that you use.

00:21:33.340 --> 00:21:35.440
So with auto-scaling,
jobs are fire and forget,

00:21:35.440 --> 00:21:38.022
and then auto-scaling can
scale up and down clusters

00:21:38.022 --> 00:21:38.980
to match your workload.

00:21:42.333 --> 00:21:44.250
So we still want to give
you low level control

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:45.660
into how auto-scaling works.

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:47.670
So importantly, you can
set the minimum cluster

00:21:47.670 --> 00:21:49.140
size and the maximum
cluster size,

00:21:49.140 --> 00:21:52.350
and you can also control how
aggressive the auto-scaler

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:54.480
responds to YARN metrics.

00:21:54.480 --> 00:21:57.567
So just to kind of dig
in, Dataproc runs YARN.

00:21:57.567 --> 00:21:59.400
It's called Yet Another
Resource Negotiator,

00:21:59.400 --> 00:22:01.200
and it's kind of the
scheduler for Hadoop.

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:04.230
And all Hadoop jobs
run on top of YARN.

00:22:04.230 --> 00:22:06.570
So if you take a MapReduce
job, for example,

00:22:06.570 --> 00:22:09.560
a MapReduce job gets split
into a set of map tasks

00:22:09.560 --> 00:22:12.690
and a set of reduce tasks, which
are then queued in YARN to be

00:22:12.690 --> 00:22:14.280
scheduled on the cluster.

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:16.230
If there's a lot of work
pending in the queue,

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:17.970
then we should scale
the cluster up.

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:20.280
But if there's a lot of free
space left in the cluster,

00:22:20.280 --> 00:22:21.905
then we should scale
it down because we

00:22:21.905 --> 00:22:24.820
have too much capacity.

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:28.565
So we allow you to choose how
to respond to these YARN metrics

00:22:28.565 --> 00:22:29.440
and how aggressively.

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:31.190
So for example, something
neat you can do,

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:33.168
is you can have very
high priority jobs,

00:22:33.168 --> 00:22:34.960
you can actually set
it so the cluster just

00:22:34.960 --> 00:22:39.115
scales up when you submit a lot
of jobs but only scales down

00:22:39.115 --> 00:22:40.990
when the cluster's idle
to avoid interrupting

00:22:40.990 --> 00:22:42.240
any jobs that are in progress.

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:47.247
So last year, we launched
auto-scaling to alpha.

00:22:47.247 --> 00:22:48.830
This year, we're
launching it to beta,

00:22:48.830 --> 00:22:50.872
and there have been a
whole slew of improvements.

00:22:50.872 --> 00:22:52.230
And I'll go through a few here.

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:54.710
So first, we've enhanced
the fine grained controls

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:56.360
that you have over auto-scaling.

00:22:56.360 --> 00:22:59.030
So for example, now you can
update auto-scaling policies

00:22:59.030 --> 00:23:02.040
at any time, and you can also
stop auto-scaling in clusters.

00:23:02.040 --> 00:23:04.710
This is especially important
for long-running clusters

00:23:04.710 --> 00:23:08.240
where you might need to tune
the auto-scaler over time.

00:23:08.240 --> 00:23:11.550
We've also focused
a lot on usability.

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:14.210
So you might have noticed
in the cluster page

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:17.400
that you now have a dashboard
of YARN in HDFS metrics.

00:23:17.400 --> 00:23:20.300
And this really helps
understand why the auto-scaler

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:22.670
is scaling up or down
based on the utilization

00:23:22.670 --> 00:23:24.273
in your cluster.

00:23:24.273 --> 00:23:26.440
And we've also put the
auto-scaling decision history

00:23:26.440 --> 00:23:29.640
in Stackdriver Logging as well.

00:23:29.640 --> 00:23:32.120
And finally, we focused
on job stability--

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:35.600
the ability to scale up and
down MapReduce and Spark

00:23:35.600 --> 00:23:37.148
jobs without having them fail.

00:23:37.148 --> 00:23:38.690
And we'll touch on
that more a little

00:23:38.690 --> 00:23:40.773
bit later, so I'm just
giving you a little teaser.

00:23:43.150 --> 00:23:45.690
So let's switch gears here and
talk about preemptible VMs.

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:48.130
So preemptible VMs are kind
of a really neat concept

00:23:48.130 --> 00:23:49.270
in Google Cloud.

00:23:49.270 --> 00:23:51.670
They're instances that
are up to 80% cheaper

00:23:51.670 --> 00:23:53.357
but can be preempted,
which really

00:23:53.357 --> 00:23:54.940
means that they're
kind of taken away,

00:23:54.940 --> 00:23:57.250
they're shut down
at any time if GCE

00:23:57.250 --> 00:24:00.660
needs to sell that
capacity elsewhere.

00:24:00.660 --> 00:24:05.850
So a lot of workloads are OK
with nodes joining and leaving,

00:24:05.850 --> 00:24:09.600
and that means that these 80%
cheaper VMs can actually make

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:12.527
it way more cost effective.

00:24:12.527 --> 00:24:14.110
So you think that
the Hadoop ecosystem

00:24:14.110 --> 00:24:16.360
is built around this idea
of nodes coming and leaving,

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:18.010
so you add a bunch
of preemptible VMs

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:19.030
to your cluster.

00:24:19.030 --> 00:24:22.120
But then, your jobs
start actually failing.

00:24:22.120 --> 00:24:24.840
So Mikayla will talk a little
bit more about why this is.

00:24:29.999 --> 00:24:31.083
MIKAYLA KONST: All right.

00:24:31.083 --> 00:24:33.625
So you added a whole bunch of
preemptible VMs to your cluster

00:24:33.625 --> 00:24:34.900
and your jobs started failing.

00:24:34.900 --> 00:24:36.950
So what's going on?

00:24:36.950 --> 00:24:41.740
So let's do a brief refresher on
the anatomy of a MapReduce job

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:43.850
to kind of understand
what's happening here.

00:24:43.850 --> 00:24:46.240
So in MapReduce,
you got your mappers

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:47.680
and you got your reducers.

00:24:47.680 --> 00:24:50.740
So you have your map tasks
running on those nodes,

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:55.540
and then your reduce
tasks need to fetch data

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:56.530
from your mappers.

00:24:56.530 --> 00:24:59.530
So they fetch the output of
the map tasks from those nodes.

00:24:59.530 --> 00:25:00.940
And so your job is going along.

00:25:00.940 --> 00:25:06.790
It's almost done, but then one
of your mappers gets preempted.

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:09.970
And as a result of this,
all those reducers that

00:25:09.970 --> 00:25:12.190
were fetching data
from that mapper,

00:25:12.190 --> 00:25:14.780
those reduce tasks also fail.

00:25:14.780 --> 00:25:20.140
So now, not only do you
have to rerun this map task,

00:25:20.140 --> 00:25:23.800
you also have to rerun all those
reduce tasks that were fetching

00:25:23.800 --> 00:25:26.110
data from that mapper.

00:25:26.110 --> 00:25:27.770
So that's kind of unfortunate.

00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:30.340
So we've actually
launched a feature

00:25:30.340 --> 00:25:34.880
to alpha that helps
mitigate this issue.

00:25:34.880 --> 00:25:40.478
So can you switch over
to the demo stream?

00:25:40.478 --> 00:25:41.710
All right.

00:25:41.710 --> 00:25:45.020
So this feature is called
Enhanced Flexibility Mode,

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:48.310
and we have it for both
MapReduce and Spark.

00:25:48.310 --> 00:25:50.595
So Karthik, can you
start that video?

00:25:50.595 --> 00:25:52.720
So first we're going to
show you an example of what

00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:54.940
happens when you don't
have Enhanced Flexibility

00:25:54.940 --> 00:25:55.990
Mode enabled.

00:25:55.990 --> 00:26:00.350
So here, I am submitting
a job to a cluster,

00:26:00.350 --> 00:26:05.290
and this is just a tiny
10-gigabyte shuffle.

00:26:05.290 --> 00:26:08.710
And this cluster does not
have Enhanced Flexibility Mode

00:26:08.710 --> 00:26:10.460
enabled.

00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:14.020
So what we're going to do
is, once this job gets going,

00:26:14.020 --> 00:26:17.470
I'm going to remove
the preemptible workers

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:19.300
from this cluster,
and we're going

00:26:19.300 --> 00:26:21.530
to see what happens
to the job progress.

00:26:21.530 --> 00:26:24.310
And it's not good.

00:26:24.310 --> 00:26:28.012
So we're just waiting
for this to get started.

00:26:28.012 --> 00:26:29.470
And then after
this, we'll show you

00:26:29.470 --> 00:26:32.630
what happens when Enhanced
Flexibility Mode is enabled,

00:26:32.630 --> 00:26:33.640
which is very exciting.

00:26:36.160 --> 00:26:38.620
All right.

00:26:38.620 --> 00:26:42.940
So we're going to switch
over to the Cluster tab here.

00:26:42.940 --> 00:26:44.080
We're editing this cluster.

00:26:44.080 --> 00:26:45.880
It used to have five
preemptible workers,

00:26:45.880 --> 00:26:49.960
and we're reducing that to zero.

00:26:49.960 --> 00:26:51.880
So now we're scaling
this cluster down,

00:26:51.880 --> 00:26:54.880
getting rid of those instances,
and let's monitor our job

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:56.960
progress to see what happens.

00:26:56.960 --> 00:26:58.360
I know that's kind
of hard to see

00:26:58.360 --> 00:27:01.750
but it looks like we're at 71%.

00:27:01.750 --> 00:27:03.940
We're chugging along.

00:27:03.940 --> 00:27:06.040
If we go back over
to the Cluster tab

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:08.740
and look at those instances,
we see those VMs are now

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:09.950
getting removed.

00:27:09.950 --> 00:27:10.690
All right.

00:27:10.690 --> 00:27:13.060
So let's check back in on
the job, and all right.

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:16.150
It went down from 75% to 21%.

00:27:16.150 --> 00:27:17.570
So all that progress was lost.

00:27:17.570 --> 00:27:21.120
You have to rerun those tasks,
which is pretty unfortunate.

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.720
So Karthik, can you
switch to the next video?

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:30.100
So now we're going to
talk about what happens.

00:27:30.100 --> 00:27:32.340
This is the exact same
setup but this cluster

00:27:32.340 --> 00:27:35.640
has Enhanced Flexibility
Mode enabled.

00:27:35.640 --> 00:27:39.600
So same set-up,
five preemptibles,

00:27:39.600 --> 00:27:42.300
two normal workers
submitting the same job,

00:27:42.300 --> 00:27:46.218
like a 10-gigabyte shuffle.

00:27:46.218 --> 00:27:47.550
Yeah.

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:50.160
And then, so with
Enhanced Flexibility Mode,

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:52.440
we actually write the
output of those map

00:27:52.440 --> 00:27:54.900
tasks to a distributed
file system

00:27:54.900 --> 00:28:00.300
so that it's check-pointed and
you don't lose that progress.

00:28:00.300 --> 00:28:03.070
So once a map task
has finished, it

00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:07.900
will never have to be
rerun again for any reason.

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:09.775
So we'll watch the job
progress for this one

00:28:09.775 --> 00:28:10.650
and see what happens.

00:28:13.303 --> 00:28:15.970
So wait for the job to get going
and then scale down the cluster

00:28:15.970 --> 00:28:17.872
again.

00:28:17.872 --> 00:28:19.330
And now it's
important to point out

00:28:19.330 --> 00:28:22.240
that in-flight map tasks
will still be killed,

00:28:22.240 --> 00:28:25.630
so you will still see
some progress lost.

00:28:25.630 --> 00:28:28.810
But all the map tasks
that are already done,

00:28:28.810 --> 00:28:30.870
that progress is saved.

00:28:30.870 --> 00:28:31.390
Right.

00:28:31.390 --> 00:28:33.610
So we're switching over
to the Cluster tab.

00:28:33.610 --> 00:28:38.440
We're going to edit the
configuration to only have

00:28:38.440 --> 00:28:40.000
zero preemptible workers.

00:28:43.860 --> 00:28:46.550
And then, again, we'll watch
what happens with that job.

00:28:50.398 --> 00:28:51.360
All right.

00:28:51.360 --> 00:28:53.565
So it looks like we're at--

00:28:53.565 --> 00:28:54.920
I can barely read that--

00:28:54.920 --> 00:28:55.420
58%?

00:28:59.770 --> 00:29:01.620
We can go see those VMs.

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:02.120
Yeah.

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:02.620
All right.

00:29:02.620 --> 00:29:05.840
The VMs are going away now.

00:29:05.840 --> 00:29:09.490
So we can check
back in on our job.

00:29:09.490 --> 00:29:10.070
All right.

00:29:10.070 --> 00:29:15.300
So this time, the progress
went from 58% to 45%.

00:29:15.300 --> 00:29:18.920
So that's like way less
dramatic than what it was before

00:29:18.920 --> 00:29:21.860
in the other one, where it
went from like 75% to 21%

00:29:21.860 --> 00:29:22.945
or something like that.

00:29:22.945 --> 00:29:24.320
And then also,
keep in mind, this

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:27.560
is a tiny 10-gigabyte job here.

00:29:27.560 --> 00:29:30.170
So for larger jobs,
the percentage

00:29:30.170 --> 00:29:34.080
of the total job of progress
that is lost is a lot smaller.

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:34.580
Yeah.

00:29:34.580 --> 00:29:38.390
So with this feature,
this is also--

00:29:38.390 --> 00:29:40.230
it works well with scaling.

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:45.230
So I guess, if you wanted to
see no job progress lost at all,

00:29:45.230 --> 00:29:47.870
you can actually use
Graceful Decommissioning.

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:49.370
So with Graceful
Decommissioning,

00:29:49.370 --> 00:29:52.700
we wait for the containers
to finish on the nodes

00:29:52.700 --> 00:29:55.070
before we remove the nodes.

00:29:55.070 --> 00:29:58.320
And usually, so with
Enhanced Flexibility Mode,

00:29:58.320 --> 00:30:00.830
this is a little bit different
than how YARN Graceful

00:30:00.830 --> 00:30:02.660
Decommissioning normally works.

00:30:02.660 --> 00:30:05.510
So with Enhanced
Flexibility Mode,

00:30:05.510 --> 00:30:08.000
as soon as those containers
are done, so as soon

00:30:08.000 --> 00:30:10.172
as those tasks are
done on those nodes,

00:30:10.172 --> 00:30:11.630
we can remove the
nodes right away,

00:30:11.630 --> 00:30:14.098
even if the application
isn't done yet.

00:30:14.098 --> 00:30:15.890
So this is different
than how YARN Graceful

00:30:15.890 --> 00:30:17.143
Decommission normally works.

00:30:17.143 --> 00:30:18.560
Because normally,
those nodes have

00:30:18.560 --> 00:30:22.250
to sit around for the entire
duration of the application

00:30:22.250 --> 00:30:24.920
because they're still
serving shuffle data.

00:30:24.920 --> 00:30:26.180
But now, they're not.

00:30:26.180 --> 00:30:28.950
Now, that shuffle data is in
a distributed file system,

00:30:28.950 --> 00:30:31.100
so we can remove those
nodes sooner and so now

00:30:31.100 --> 00:30:35.360
scaling could be
more responsive.

00:30:35.360 --> 00:30:39.760
And I'll hand it over
to Chris, I think.

00:30:39.760 --> 00:30:46.490
We can switch back to
the questions slide.

00:30:46.490 --> 00:30:47.660
Can we switch back to--

00:30:50.195 --> 00:30:52.195
CHRIS CROSBIE: Do you
want to advance one slide?

00:30:52.195 --> 00:30:54.884
MIKAYLA KONST: Can
you advance the slide?

00:30:54.884 --> 00:30:55.690
Oh.

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:56.190
Oops.

00:30:56.190 --> 00:30:56.690
OK.

00:30:56.690 --> 00:30:57.312
I guess I can.

00:30:57.312 --> 00:30:58.270
CHRIS CROSBIE: So yeah.

00:30:58.270 --> 00:31:00.080
That's the end of the
session, everybody.

