is not yet enhanced-ly flexible.

00:35:24.626 --> 00:35:26.292
AUDIENCE: My question
is more around,

00:35:26.292 --> 00:35:28.000
like, let's say there's
skew in your data

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:32.090
and then Spark recognizes that
and dynamically repartitions

00:35:32.090 --> 00:35:32.590
it.

00:35:32.590 --> 00:35:34.120
So you're not
touching any of that?

00:35:34.120 --> 00:35:34.220
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: No.

00:35:34.220 --> 00:35:34.460
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:35:34.460 --> 00:35:35.140
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN:
So, yeah, if you

00:35:35.140 --> 00:35:36.918
turn on cost-based
optimization 2.3,

00:35:36.918 --> 00:35:38.710
then yeah, then that
should all still work.

00:35:38.710 --> 00:35:39.350
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:35:39.350 --> 00:35:39.850
Cool.

00:35:39.850 --> 00:35:40.350
Thank you.

00:35:40.350 --> 00:35:42.850
CHRIS CROSBIE: And then when
there are overlaps with this,

00:35:42.850 --> 00:35:44.350
like, for instance, I
know Mikayla brought up

00:35:44.350 --> 00:35:46.180
Graceful Decommissioning,
that is something

00:35:46.180 --> 00:35:48.460
that we're trying to change
in the open-source Spark

00:35:48.460 --> 00:35:49.720
and push upstream.

00:35:49.720 --> 00:35:51.227
So when we do hit
the overlaps, we

00:35:51.227 --> 00:35:53.060
are trying to work with
the community on it.

00:35:55.712 --> 00:35:57.780
AUDIENCE: My question
is, I wanted to ask you

00:35:57.780 --> 00:36:00.420
about Graceful Decommissioning.

00:36:00.420 --> 00:36:02.970
Is it not fulfilling
the proposed

00:36:02.970 --> 00:36:04.560
that you have presented already?

00:36:07.440 --> 00:36:11.440
When you configure
Graceful Decommissioning,

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:17.115
is it not waiting until the
map job is done before--

00:36:17.115 --> 00:36:18.240
MIKAYLA KONST: So, it does.

00:36:18.240 --> 00:36:21.810
So right now, if you have
Enhanced Flexibility Mode

00:36:21.810 --> 00:36:24.390
enabled, so we actually
made some changes

00:36:24.390 --> 00:36:26.580
to Graceful Decommissioning
so that it works better

00:36:26.580 --> 00:36:28.050
with Enhanced Flexibility Mode.

00:36:28.050 --> 00:36:30.720
So it waits for
those map tasks to be

00:36:30.720 --> 00:36:34.000
done before decommissioning
those nodes.

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:35.430
So what I was
showing in the demo,

00:36:35.430 --> 00:36:37.500
I actually didn't use
Graceful Decommissioning,

00:36:37.500 --> 00:36:39.360
and that's why we saw
some progress lost,

00:36:39.360 --> 00:36:41.130
even with Enhanced
Flexibility Mode on,

00:36:41.130 --> 00:36:44.153
because we didn't wait for those
in-flight containers to finish.

00:36:44.153 --> 00:36:45.570
But with Graceful
Decommissioning,

00:36:45.570 --> 00:36:47.190
we wait for the
containers to finish

00:36:47.190 --> 00:36:49.200
but we don't wait for
the entire application

00:36:49.200 --> 00:36:52.110
to finish because now we don't
need to because the shuffle

00:36:52.110 --> 00:36:53.985
data is preserved.

00:36:53.985 --> 00:36:55.360
The nodes don't
have to serve it.

00:36:55.360 --> 00:36:57.386
Did that answer the question?

00:36:57.386 --> 00:36:58.940
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:36:58.940 --> 00:36:59.940
I will have a look.

00:36:59.940 --> 00:37:01.240
Thank you.

00:37:01.240 --> 00:37:07.920
And I also wanted to say to
you that I have tried the HBase

00:37:07.920 --> 00:37:09.210
initialization action.

00:37:09.210 --> 00:37:10.615
It works really well.

00:37:10.615 --> 00:37:12.426
MIKAYLA KONST: Oh good.

00:37:12.426 --> 00:37:16.800
AUDIENCE: And I think that it's
also really well-documented.

00:37:16.800 --> 00:37:21.240
I just wanted to say, I think
that maybe adding a dropdown

00:37:21.240 --> 00:37:24.830
menu just below the
initialization action, where

00:37:24.830 --> 00:37:28.740
you can quickly choose
the HBase, or Ranger,

00:37:28.740 --> 00:37:34.500
or whatever you like,
might be a little more

00:37:34.500 --> 00:37:37.380
appealing than having to
pull from cloud storage

00:37:37.380 --> 00:37:38.440
and create a--

00:37:38.440 --> 00:37:39.960
MIKAYLA KONST: Oh yeah.

00:37:39.960 --> 00:37:41.080
I totally agree.

00:37:41.080 --> 00:37:41.580
So there's--

00:37:41.580 --> 00:37:42.045
AUDIENCE: --a suggestion.

00:37:42.045 --> 00:37:42.878
MIKAYLA KONST: Yeah.

00:37:42.878 --> 00:37:44.378
There's stuff in
the works for that.

00:37:44.378 --> 00:37:45.461
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: Yeah.

00:37:45.461 --> 00:37:47.310
That's excellent feedback
and we are on it.

00:37:47.310 --> 00:37:47.790
MIKAYLA KONST: Yeah.

00:37:47.790 --> 00:37:48.450
AUDIENCE: Thank you, guys.

00:37:48.450 --> 00:37:49.410
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN:
Hopefully by the next Next,

00:37:49.410 --> 00:37:50.202
that will be there.

00:37:50.202 --> 00:37:52.515
MIKAYLA KONST: Yeah.

00:37:52.515 --> 00:37:54.900
AUDIENCE: First
question is, do you

00:37:54.900 --> 00:37:58.910
have any plans to expand
the pre-built image list?

00:37:58.910 --> 00:38:01.640
Right now, like if you
look at these options,

00:38:01.640 --> 00:38:10.970
it's like comparing to a
general execution machine,

00:38:10.970 --> 00:38:13.020
you have a long list of options.

00:38:13.020 --> 00:38:15.870
But if you go Dataproc,
it's maybe like five,

00:38:15.870 --> 00:38:19.630
six different options
off a pre-built image.

00:38:19.630 --> 00:38:21.017
Do have any plan to support--

00:38:21.017 --> 00:38:22.100
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: Yeah.

00:38:22.100 --> 00:38:23.505
We tried to address this--

00:38:23.505 --> 00:38:25.130
I think there's more
than five or six--

00:38:25.130 --> 00:38:29.150
but we do try to address this
with custom images, where

00:38:29.150 --> 00:38:30.862
you do have the
capability to come in,

00:38:30.862 --> 00:38:32.570
put whatever you would
like on the image.

00:38:32.570 --> 00:38:35.170
Oftentimes, enterprises with
strict security controls

00:38:35.170 --> 00:38:37.100
will put their own
security constraints on it.

00:38:37.100 --> 00:38:38.808
Or you might have some
additional version

00:38:38.808 --> 00:38:40.595
that you want to
upgrade independently.

00:38:40.595 --> 00:38:42.470
You have the ability to
do that, and then you

00:38:42.470 --> 00:38:43.940
can create a custom
image and then

00:38:43.940 --> 00:38:45.385
come back and use that image.

00:38:45.385 --> 00:38:46.760
So we do give you
the flexibility

00:38:46.760 --> 00:38:49.465
to do what you need
to do with the image.

00:38:49.465 --> 00:38:52.910
AUDIENCE: So even without
any customization,

00:38:52.910 --> 00:38:56.930
do you have any plan to support
CentOS and different kind

00:38:56.930 --> 00:38:58.058
of operating system?

00:38:58.058 --> 00:39:00.600
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: So we just
added Ubuntu because we got--

00:39:00.600 --> 00:39:01.080
AUDIENCE: I saw that.

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:01.926
Yes.

00:39:01.926 --> 00:39:04.330
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: --just
based on customer demand.

00:39:04.330 --> 00:39:06.453
So if you do have
use cases for that,

00:39:06.453 --> 00:39:08.870
I'd love to talk to you about
it and see if it makes sense

00:39:08.870 --> 00:39:12.622
to do additional images because
that's where Ubuntu came from,

00:39:12.622 --> 00:39:14.330
it was just customers
were asking for it.

00:39:14.330 --> 00:39:14.872
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:39:14.872 --> 00:39:16.072
Yeah.

00:39:16.072 --> 00:39:17.780
CHRIS CROSBIE: As
well, a little bit more

00:39:17.780 --> 00:39:19.250
background, Ubuntu is
also very straightforward

00:39:19.250 --> 00:39:21.950
because it's the same packages
between [INAUDIBLE] and Ubuntu.

00:39:21.950 --> 00:39:24.890
So that's another reason that
CentOS is potentially more

00:39:24.890 --> 00:39:28.000
difficult to do.

00:39:28.000 --> 00:39:31.080
AUDIENCE: We are looking
for CentOS support.

00:39:31.080 --> 00:39:31.950
CHRIS CROSBIE: OK.

00:39:31.950 --> 00:39:32.450
Cool.

00:39:32.450 --> 00:39:32.990
Yeah.

00:39:32.990 --> 00:39:34.880
AUDIENCE: The next
question is, what's

00:39:34.880 --> 00:39:38.560
the difference between the
templates and Composer?

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:40.785
Is there any feature
function overlap?

00:39:40.785 --> 00:39:42.410
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN:
There's actually--

00:39:42.410 --> 00:39:44.180
before I'll let them talk more.

00:39:44.180 --> 00:39:47.150
But there actually is an
integration point between them,

00:39:47.150 --> 00:39:51.210
so Cloud Composer does have a
workflow templates operator.

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:56.420
So you can drop into Composer
this operator, and that

00:39:56.420 --> 00:39:58.490
will do all of your
Dataproc jobs and cluster

00:39:58.490 --> 00:40:01.580
all as one black box
entity within Composer.

00:40:01.580 --> 00:40:03.020
So there is an
integration point,

00:40:03.020 --> 00:40:04.437
and that's how
customers typically

00:40:04.437 --> 00:40:05.790
work together with them.

00:40:05.790 --> 00:40:06.870
I don't if you want
to add more to that.

00:40:06.870 --> 00:40:07.190
MIKAYLA KONST: Yeah.

00:40:07.190 --> 00:40:09.565
I mean, I was basically just
going to say the same thing.

00:40:09.565 --> 00:40:15.050
Like, a Dataproc workflow can
be like one node in a Composer,

00:40:15.050 --> 00:40:18.470
like a bigger Composer
orchestration.

00:40:18.470 --> 00:40:22.610
So Composer is
integrated with all sorts

00:40:22.610 --> 00:40:24.470
of different things in GCP.

00:40:24.470 --> 00:40:29.640
Workflow templates are kind of
our Dataproc-native workflows.

00:40:29.640 --> 00:40:31.670
So you could do the
same thing in Composer,

00:40:31.670 --> 00:40:33.500
but it's kind of
annoying in Composer

00:40:33.500 --> 00:40:35.900
to have to be like, OK,
let me create a cluster,

00:40:35.900 --> 00:40:38.630
and then submit these jobs,
and then tear down the cluster,

00:40:38.630 --> 00:40:41.005
like all these nodes in the
graph, where, logically, it's

00:40:41.005 --> 00:40:42.000
just one operation.

00:40:42.000 --> 00:40:48.010
So Dataproc does this part
of my overall pipeline.

00:40:48.010 --> 00:40:50.353
AUDIENCE: So then,
another two question then.

00:40:50.353 --> 00:40:51.395
MIKAYLA KONST: Yes, sure.

00:40:51.395 --> 00:40:53.690
AUDIENCE: Is there
any-- like, say,

00:40:53.690 --> 00:40:57.120
I don't want to have two
different templates over there.

00:40:57.120 --> 00:40:59.390
One is built on
top of the Airflow,

00:40:59.390 --> 00:41:02.800
the other is built on
top of the templates.

00:41:02.800 --> 00:41:09.327
Is there any way I can use the
same templates in Composer?

00:41:09.327 --> 00:41:10.160
MIKAYLA KONST: Yeah.

00:41:10.160 --> 00:41:14.090
So there's a workflow operator,
like a Dataproc workflow

00:41:14.090 --> 00:41:16.070
operator, in Airflow.

00:41:16.070 --> 00:41:18.560
So you can use your
same workflow templates

00:41:18.560 --> 00:41:21.240
from Dataproc in Composer.

00:41:21.240 --> 00:41:21.782
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:41:21.782 --> 00:41:24.032
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: And one
of the things to consider

00:41:24.032 --> 00:41:25.623
is that Dataproc
workflow is really

00:41:25.623 --> 00:41:27.040
just a convenient
way of saying, I

00:41:27.040 --> 00:41:28.730
want to run these
jobs in this cluster.

00:41:28.730 --> 00:41:30.620
But there's no actual
native scheduling.

00:41:30.620 --> 00:41:32.810
If you want to say, run
this job twice a day,

00:41:32.810 --> 00:41:34.390
or when data arrives in GCS--

00:41:34.390 --> 00:41:36.140
AUDIENCE: But still,
you have some overlap

00:41:36.140 --> 00:41:39.397
to function between templates
and the Composer, right?

00:41:39.397 --> 00:41:40.480
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: Yeah.

00:41:40.480 --> 00:41:43.670
AUDIENCE: That's
only if these two are

00:41:43.670 --> 00:41:46.370
totally exchangeable
and compatible,

00:41:46.370 --> 00:41:47.570
I might take their approach.

00:41:47.570 --> 00:41:50.575
Otherwise, I will stay
with Composer, right?

00:41:50.575 --> 00:41:51.950
CHRIS CROSBIE: It
just comes down

00:41:51.950 --> 00:41:55.530
to our general theory about, we
try to expose a lot of knobs.

00:41:55.530 --> 00:41:57.920
And so you want to turn
the more detailed knobs,

00:41:57.920 --> 00:41:59.810
you can do that
directly in Composer.

00:41:59.810 --> 00:42:01.310
You want to abstract
that all away

00:42:01.310 --> 00:42:05.690
and treat it as one workflow
template box, you can do that.

00:42:05.690 --> 00:42:07.260
And that way, you
might even have,

00:42:07.260 --> 00:42:09.068
depending on what
organization you're in,

00:42:09.068 --> 00:42:11.360
there might be somebody that
does the Dataproc workflow

00:42:11.360 --> 00:42:13.790
templates, and then it can
hand off that entire black box

00:42:13.790 --> 00:42:17.120
to the person doing Cloud
Composer or Airflow workflow.

00:42:17.120 --> 00:42:19.650
So it's just sort of, where do
you want to turn those knobs?

00:42:19.650 --> 00:42:23.540
AUDIENCE: I definitely see the
value of the templates here,

00:42:23.540 --> 00:42:26.940
but I just don't want to
have two different versions

00:42:26.940 --> 00:42:31.015
of templates-- one in
Dataproc kind of templates

00:42:31.015 --> 00:42:32.377
and the Composer--

00:42:32.377 --> 00:42:33.460
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: Sure.

00:42:33.460 --> 00:42:35.060
And feel free to
come talk to us later

00:42:35.060 --> 00:42:36.880
about your use case as well.

00:42:36.880 --> 00:42:39.688
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:42:39.688 --> 00:42:40.630
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:42:40.630 --> 00:42:44.810
For the Spark
BigQuery connector,

00:42:44.810 --> 00:42:47.363
you guys removed the
intermediate storage when

00:42:47.363 --> 00:42:50.030
you're reading, but did you also
remove that for writing as well

00:42:50.030 --> 00:42:51.710
or just writing
is still writes--

00:42:51.710 --> 00:42:53.335
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN:
So right now, this

00:42:53.335 --> 00:42:54.920
is just the read connector.

00:42:54.920 --> 00:42:59.540
So there's not a similar
write connector yet.

00:42:59.540 --> 00:43:03.300
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] like the
other thing is like the write

00:43:03.300 --> 00:43:04.858
character is whenever--

00:43:04.858 --> 00:43:06.150
it's a two-step process, right?

00:43:06.150 --> 00:43:07.775
So yes, you're writing
to GCS, and then

00:43:07.775 --> 00:43:10.980
GCS loads into BigQuery, but
sometimes the load fails.

00:43:10.980 --> 00:43:13.710
But your connector
doesn't retry [INAUDIBLE],,

00:43:13.710 --> 00:43:15.890
like we'll have to see.

00:43:15.890 --> 00:43:17.730
Is there a way for you to retry?

00:43:17.730 --> 00:43:20.035
Because it's part of
your connector, right?

00:43:20.035 --> 00:43:22.563
KARTHIK PALANIAPPAN: Yeah.

00:43:22.563 --> 00:43:24.480
A lot of the libraries
have low-level retries.

00:43:24.480 --> 00:43:26.440
But, yeah, that's
something to look into.

00:43:26.440 --> 00:43:27.857
Again, our connector
is on GitHub,

00:43:27.857 --> 00:43:30.720
so if you found issue there,
we'll also take a look.

00:43:30.720 --> 00:43:31.894
So yeah.

00:43:35.870 --> 00:43:36.870
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:43:36.870 --> 00:43:38.760
So this is more of
a follow-up question

00:43:38.760 --> 00:43:42.277
on Graceful Decommissioning
and the flexible scheduling.

00:43:42.277 --> 00:43:43.360
MIKAYLA KONST: Yeah, sure.

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:47.130
AUDIENCE: So if
Graceful Decommissioning

00:43:47.130 --> 00:43:51.000
will wait until the completion
of the task regardless, right,

00:43:51.000 --> 00:43:55.680
so in the original version, when
you saw that 50% drop, if you

00:43:55.680 --> 00:43:57.750
had Graceful
Decommissioning, would you

00:43:57.750 --> 00:43:59.040
have seen any drop at all?

00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:02.670
MIKAYLA KONST: So we wouldn't
have, but it's kind of a pain

00:44:02.670 --> 00:44:05.400
because the nodes wouldn't
be removed from the cluster

00:44:05.400 --> 00:44:07.860
until the application
is completely done.

00:44:07.860 --> 00:44:11.373
So I guess another big benefit
of the Enhanced Flexibility

00:44:11.373 --> 00:44:13.290
Mode is you can remove
nodes from your cluster

00:44:13.290 --> 00:44:15.720
faster so you're not paying
for them when all you're doing

00:44:15.720 --> 00:44:17.220
is serving shuffle data.

00:44:17.220 --> 00:44:17.980
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:44:17.980 --> 00:44:19.320
Is there like--

00:44:19.320 --> 00:44:20.570
MIKAYLA KONST: And it's also--

00:44:20.570 --> 00:44:22.050
AUDIENCE: --a breakdown on--

00:44:22.050 --> 00:44:22.570
sorry.

00:44:22.570 --> 00:44:22.950
MIKAYLA KONST: Go ahead.

00:44:22.950 --> 00:44:24.870
AUDIENCE: I was
considering like, if that's

00:44:24.870 --> 00:44:27.780
the main benefit, this
new feature in the sense

00:44:27.780 --> 00:44:30.540
that you can tear
down faster, do

00:44:30.540 --> 00:44:33.540
you have any kind of numbers
on what the distribution, what

00:44:33.540 --> 00:44:38.278
the benefit is of a job to its
shuffle time and to the cost?

00:44:38.278 --> 00:44:40.820
MIKAYLA KONST: So I don't have
exact numbers on me right now.

00:44:40.820 --> 00:44:42.705
AUDIENCE: Well,
just a general idea.

00:44:42.705 --> 00:44:45.990
MIKAYLA KONST: So,
I guess, again,

00:44:45.990 --> 00:44:49.920
for small or medium-sized
shuffles, the times of the jobs

00:44:49.920 --> 00:44:52.080
are comparable, I guess.

00:44:52.080 --> 00:44:55.090
And then for larger
shuffles, sometimes--

00:44:55.090 --> 00:44:56.850
especially if you're
using HDFS, there

00:44:56.850 --> 00:44:59.910
can be some bottle-necking
issues with the NameNode.

00:44:59.910 --> 00:45:02.070
And you can tune the
NameNode to avoid those.

00:45:02.070 --> 00:45:05.850
If you've use GCS, you don't
see those issues as much.

00:45:05.850 --> 00:45:06.350
But, yeah.

00:45:06.350 --> 00:45:07.808
CHRIS CROSBIE:
Essentially, this is

00:45:07.808 --> 00:45:10.350
why we decided to make
this feature a public alpha

00:45:10.350 --> 00:45:13.050
versus a EAP or a private
alpha because we want

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:15.240
as many people
testing it as possible

00:45:15.240 --> 00:45:17.010
so we can start to
do these benchmarks

00:45:17.010 --> 00:45:18.480
and figure out best practices.

00:45:18.480 --> 00:45:21.670
And that way, when we do
hit EA, which is, hey,

00:45:21.670 --> 00:45:24.210
we really trust this, that's
going to become coupled with,

00:45:24.210 --> 00:45:26.520
and here is the type of
cost savings you can expect

00:45:26.520 --> 00:45:28.560
or here is the kind of
trade-offs you can expect

00:45:28.560 --> 00:45:29.810
with these different settings.

00:45:29.810 --> 00:45:32.753
And so we do plan to get more
crisp on a lot of that stuff.

00:45:32.753 --> 00:45:34.920
But right now, it's just
about getting it out there,

00:45:34.920 --> 00:45:37.207
getting folks using it,
and starting to test out

00:45:37.207 --> 00:45:38.040
a lot of this stuff.

00:45:38.040 --> 00:45:40.590
MIKAYLA KONST: And
then again, one

00:45:40.590 --> 00:45:42.450
of the huge reasons
we did this feature

00:45:42.450 --> 00:45:45.430
is to help you
with cost savings.

00:45:45.430 --> 00:45:47.910
So I guess, if you want to
use a lot of preemptible VMs--

00:45:47.910 --> 00:45:49.590
so I guess, in the
demo, I showed me

00:45:49.590 --> 00:45:51.472
purposely removing
the preemptible VMs,

00:45:51.472 --> 00:45:53.430
but you don't know when
the preemptible VMs are

00:45:53.430 --> 00:45:54.610
going to go away.

00:45:54.610 --> 00:45:57.175
So when the preemptible
VMs get preempted,

00:45:57.175 --> 00:45:59.300
there's no Graceful
Decommissioning there, I guess.

00:45:59.300 --> 00:46:01.440
So I guess that's why
I demoed what I did.

00:46:01.440 --> 00:46:04.292
AUDIENCE: Oh, so if a
preemptible gets preempted,

00:46:04.292 --> 00:46:06.000
it still doesn't
gracefully decommission?

00:46:06.000 --> 00:46:06.780
MIKAYLA KONST: It does not.

00:46:06.780 --> 00:46:07.020
No.

00:46:07.020 --> 00:46:07.290
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:46:07.290 --> 00:46:08.247
MIKAYLA KONST: Yeah.

00:46:08.247 --> 00:46:09.330
So it's to help with that.

00:46:09.330 --> 00:46:10.010
AUDIENCE: That makes sense.

00:46:10.010 --> 00:46:10.415
